  "subtitle": "A walking machine in an ordinary working world",
  "summary": "Steambot Chronicles is Irem's PlayStation 2 action role-playing game, first released in Japan on 30 June 2005. It imagines an industrial age where Trotmobiles, walking machines used for transport and labour, have become part of daily life. The player can travel, take jobs, customise a machine and become involved with musicians and other inhabitants. The adventure leaves room between its central events for those smaller activities. A machine that can fight is also a vehicle for finding work and reaching the next place, giving the world a practical, lived-in character.",
  "body": [
    "Steambot Chronicles is Irem's PlayStation 2 action role-playing game, first released in Japan on 30 June 2005. It imagines an industrial age where Trotmobiles, walking machines used for transport and labour, have become part of daily life. The player can travel, take jobs, customise a machine and become involved with musicians and other inhabitants. The adventure leaves room between its central events for those smaller activities. A machine that can fight is also a vehicle for finding work and reaching the next place, giving the world a practical, lived-in character.",
    "Different activities use different parts of the game's systems. Performing music becomes a rhythm game, mechanical adjustments change a Trotmobile and trading connects travel to the local economy. The player moves between them rather than remaining inside one uninterrupted combat campaign. These choices give a visit to town several possible purposes: earn money, meet someone or prepare for another journey. The sandbox structure allows the character's life to gather around the larger plot, making an apparently small detour part of the way the player comes to know the setting.",
    "Kazuma Kujo produced the game for Irem. North American publisher Atlus released it in 2006, with 505 Games handling PAL territories later that year. A larger sequel was announced and shown in 2006 but eventually cancelled in 2011, leaving the original as the fullest expression of this particular world. Smaller related games explored other parts of the concept. Steambot Chronicles remains distinctive for allowing the same machine to belong to work, travel and adventure, while music and everyday transactions give the player reasons to spend time outside a battle."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "A walking machine in an ordinary working world",
    "Let the journey become something else",
    "An open-ended idea with a short series"
  ],
